7-simplicial cycle sequences for some $\\sigma$σ 8 (Kottari et al., 25 Aug 2025). This is one of the clearest demonstrations that higher-dimensional tree theory cannot be reduced to a single naïve extension of graph-theoretic counting formulas.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Azhad_Q in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3AAzhad_Q+in%3Amath)\nciteturn3search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; @misc{2508.17854, author = {Gaurav Kottari and Niteesh Sahni and Qazi J. ... Focusing on the case m = n-1 in Dewdney's (m, n)-tree framework, we introduce refined notions of path and circuit sequences that overcome the structural limitations of existing definitions. ... These results provide a structurally complete theory of (n-1, n)-trees, parallel to the classical theory of graph-theoretic trees.\n  * Structural Characterisations of (n-1,n)-Trees\n\nGaurav Kottari, Niteesh Sahni, Qazi J. Azhad\n\nAug 26 2025 math.CO arXiv:2508.17854v2\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2508.17854, author = {Gaurav Kottari and Niteesh Sahni and Qazi J.~Azhad}, title = {{S}tructural {C}haracterisations of (n-1,n)-{T}rees}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2508.17854}, note = {arXiv:2508.17854v2} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nWe study higher-dimensional analogues of graph-theoretic trees within the class of pure n-simplicial complexes. Focusing on the case m = n-1 in Dewdney's (m, n)-tree framework, we introduce refined notions of path and circuit sequences that overcome the structural limitations of existing definitions. Using these refinements, we establish higher-dimensional analogues of the classical characterisations of trees in graphs, including equivalences based on connectivity, acyclicity, path uniqueness, and enumerative constraints. We further disprove two conjectures posed by Dewdney by constructing explicit counterexamples, and we formulate corrected versions that hold under additional necessary conditions in the case m = n-1. These results provide a structurally complete theory of (n-1, n)-trees, parallel to the classical theory of graph-theoretic trees.\n\n  * Bayesian estimation of Unit-Weibull distribution based on dual generalized order statistics with application to the Cotton Production Data\n\nQazi J. Azhad, Abdul Nasir Khan, Bhagwati Devi, Jahangir Sabbir Khan, Ayush Tripathi\n\nFeb 06 2025 stat.ME math.ST stat.OT stat.TH arXiv:2502.02927v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2502.02927, author = {Qazi J.~Azhad and Abdul Nasir Khan and Bhagwati Devi and Jahangir Sabbir Khan and Ayush Tripathi}, title = {{B}ayesian estimation of {U}nit-{W}eibull distribution based on dual generalized order statistics with application to the {C}otton {P}roduction {D}ata}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2502.02927}, note = {arXiv:2502.02927v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Kitson_D in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3AKitson_D+in%3Amath)\nciteturn3search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; @misc{2508.00134, author = {James Cruickshank and Sean Dewar and Derek Kitson}, title = {{A}lgebraic connectivity in normed spaces}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2508.00134}, note = {arXiv:2508.00134v1} }[Button: Copy Citation] ... The algebraic connectivity of a graph $G$ in a finite dimensional real normed linear space $X$ is a geometric counterpart to the Fiedler number of the graph and can be regarded as a measure of the rigidity of the graph in $X$.\n# Search SciRate\n\n[Input]\n\nSearch query[Input]\n\nAdvanced\n\nAuthor(s)  | [Input]  | Comma-separated author names\nTitle  | [Input]  | Contents of paper title\nAbstract  | [Input]  | Contents of paper abstract\nCategory  | [Input]  | Search a particular arXiv category\nScited By  | [Input]  | Papers scited by a particular user\nDate Published  | [Select]  | Search within a given date range\nOrder By  | [Select]  | How to sort the results\n\n\n22 results for au:Kitson_D in:math\n\nShow all abstracts\n\n  * Algebraic connectivity in normed spaces\n\nJames Cruickshank, Sean Dewar, Derek Kitson\n\nAug 04 2025 math.CO math.MG math.SP arXiv:2508.00134v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2508.00134, author = {James Cruickshank and Sean Dewar and Derek Kitson}, title = {{A}lgebraic connectivity in normed spaces}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2508.00134}, note = {arXiv:2508.00134v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nThe algebraic connectivity of a graph $G$ in a finite dimensional real normed linear space $X$ is a geometric counterpart to the Fiedler number of the graph and can be regarded as a measure of the rigidity of the graph in $X$. We analyse the behaviour of the algebraic connectivity of $G$ in $X$ with respect to graph decomposition, vertex deletion and isometric isomorphism, and provide a general bound expressed in terms of the geometry of $X$ and the Fiedler number of the graph. Particular focus is given to the space $\\ell_\\infty^d$ where we present explicit formulae and calculations as well as upper and lower bounds. As a key tool, we show that the monochrome subgraphs of a complete framework in $\\ell_\\infty^d$ are odd-hole-free. Connections to redundant rigidity are also presented.\n\n  * The Rigid Unit Mode spectrum for symmetric frameworks\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMathematics Aug 2025 (https://borowiecki.dev/list/math/2025-08)\nciteturn3search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Title: Algebraic connectivity in normed spaces\n\nJames Cruickshank, Sean Dewar, Derek Kitson ... 1-20 (2026)\n[23] arXiv:2508.00134 [pdf, html, other]\n    \n\nTitle: Algebraic connectivity in normed spaces\n\nJames Cruickshank, Sean Dewar, Derek Kitson\n\nSubjects: Combinatorics (math.CO); Metric Geometry (math.MG); Spectral Theory (math.SP)\n\n[24] arXiv:2508.00136 [pdf, html, other]\n    \n\nTitle: Uniq